  "body": "**Stacked on #381** (#368, tool bodies from STEP). This diff includes #381's commits until it merges; the #369 work is 99273c8 plus a docs fix. Part of #361: the lathe case the issue asks for (Capto/KM holders, boring bars).\n\n## What changed\n**Lathe tools can carry a body.** Turning, Groove, Thread, Form and Bore tools accept a body through `SetToolBody::lathe` / `lathe_from_file`. The placement is nine numbers:\n- the model direction that is tool +x (radially out, on the path's own meridian side);\n- the model direction that is +z (lathe +Z);\n- the model point that is the tip (the point the moves already carry).\n\nDirections within 0.01° of square are squared up; anything worse is refused by name. The frame is derived from the existing turning code and documented in `docs/decisions/369-lathe-holder-body.md`.\n\n**Clearance bands** (`crates/tools/src/lathe_holder.rs`):\n- The body is cut into bands of at least 0.25 mm along z. Each facet is clipped exactly to each band, which keeps the x and |y| bounds of what it holds.\n- With the tip at signed radius X, every body point in the band lies between a radial floor and a ceiling (formulas in the decisions file).\n- The ceiling matters for a boring bar, whose far side or flats rub the bore wall; \"min x, ignore y\" would miss it.\n- The bounds are a box around the body's points, so they can only over-report.\n\n**The judge** (`operation/turning_holder*.rs`):\n- **Moves:** every feed and rapid is walked at 0.25 mm steps.\n- **Reading the material:** each band is read against a station table of the part and the in-process stock (outline knots, facet vertices, a 0.25 mm grid) at its ends and every station inside it, so the skins are read exactly.\n- **What stands:** the stock counts less every cut before that instant, by the same reading as the bar law (#146): earlier ops and pilots, the program's facing, this path's earlier cuts, and this move's own sweep so far. The insert's own footprint is excluded.\n- **Finding:** a rub is a new `Warning::HolderHit` that **blocks the post**: `<op>: holder body at move N rubs the part - X mm short of clearance`.\n\n**Unchanged without a body.** A turning tool without a body gives identical moves, warnings and goldens. It gains one non-blocking path note, \"holder not judged\".\n\n**Project file.** No version bump beyond #381's v55. A lathe body is written as `lathe: {radial, along, tip}` with no `axis`/`offset`, so a #381-only reader refuses the file rather than misreading it. The clearance bands are re-derived on load.\n\n## How tested\n- New `crates/app/tests/turning_holder.rs` (8):\n  - OD holder rubs a stepped part's shoulder: part hit, 3.2 mm short, post refused. The same op without a body is identical.\n  - A clear holder passes.\n  - Uncut bar under the holder is a stock-only hit; a rough op first clears it.\n  - A fat boring bar is a part hit on rapids and feeds; a slim bar passes.\n  - A grooving holder 0.05 mm inside the blade band passes; a fat one rubs the walls.\n  - Refusals by name: zero, non-square or NaN placement, tip far from the body, empty or flat body, a lathe body on an end mill.\n  - Undo; save → load → save byte-identical.\n- Unit tests:\n  - Tools (6): a randomized check that every facet point sits within its band's radii; facets crossing bands with no vertex in them; the placement mapping.\n  - App scene (2): the station-line cut against brute force for a capsule, a disc, a hexagon and a cut through the axis.\n- `scripts/gate.sh quick upstream/main` (macOS): tools, sim and app suites green. Every turning, lathe, golden, project and undo suite is unchanged. In the ui suites, only the three headless-GPU tests fail (no graphics adapter on this machine); they fail the same way on `upstream/main`.\n- `cargo clippy -p camcraft-tools -p camcraft-sim -p camcraft-app -p camcraft-ui --tests` is clean.\n\n## Not exercised\n- No UI: attaching a lathe body is reachable through the command and the project file only (#371).\n- No real vendor Capto/KM file was tried, only test meshes. Real vendor files are often large, and the judge's work grows with instants × band runs (see the decisions file).\n\nChangelog: Turning tools can carry a modelled holder or boring-bar body; a body that rubs the part or in-process stock blocks the post by name (#369)\n\nFixes #369\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
